    <p>
      Modifying components based on evaluating the previous output, step by step, is the core of
      prompt refinement.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Remember">
      <p>
        Refinement means changing one or a few components at a time based on the output you see.
        Missing information → Context. Wrong tone → Role. Wrong action or limits → Task. Wrong
        structure → Output Format. And if you need a behavior, say it explicitly; an example alone
        may not be enough.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>


    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Checklist <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="When an output isn’t what you expected:"
      items={[
        '<strong>Find the component at fault</strong> — missing facts → Context; wrong behavior → Task, Examples, or Output Format',
        '<strong>Change one component at a time</strong> — so you know what made the difference',
        '<strong>Re-run on the same inputs</strong> — compare old and new prompt versions side by side',
        '<strong>Run more than once</strong> — outputs vary between runs',
        '<strong>State required behavior explicitly</strong> — don’t rely on an example alone (the ALL CAPS test)',
        '<strong>Give enough context, not too much</strong> — drop irrelevant info and conflicting instructions',
        '<strong>For agents: clear tool descriptions</strong> — they go into the context the model decides from',
        '<strong>Validate critical outputs</strong> — models can hallucinate and reflect bias',
        '<strong>Leave no gap to interpret</strong> — length, audience, format, and what to do when information is missing',
      ]}
    />

